In the programming language Rust, trait objects are a way to create objects that implement a trait, which is a set of behaviors that the object must implement. Trait objects are created by calling a function that returns a reference to the trait object, and then using that reference to call the methods of the trait on the object.

Traits are a way to define a set of behaviors that a type should implement, but it is not a concrete type. Trait objects are a way to create an instance of a trait, which is a concrete object that can be used like any other object in the program.

For example, you can define a `Trait` struct and then implement the methods of the trait on the struct:
```
struct Trait<T> {
    value: T,
}

impl<T> Trait<T> for Trait<T> {
    fn method(&self) {
        println!("Method called on Trait<T>");
    }
}
```
Then you can create an instance of the trait object and use it like this:
```
let my_trait = Trait::<i32>(42);
my_trait.method(); // prints "Method called on Trait<i32>"
```
Trait objects are an important feature of Rust because they allow you to create objects that implement a trait, which can be used like any other object in the program. This makes it easy to create and use objects that have a specific set of behaviors, and it can help you write more modular and reusable code.
